# A fold that allocates a temporary each time round and threads a map. The
# temporary is never stored: it is bound, its length is read, and it is
# dropped. Threading an int instead, the same loop reclaims every one of them.
#
# Threading a map it reclaims none, because the cluster carries no beat
# bracket at all and so has no rewind point anywhere in it. The numbers below
# are that: allocs and arena blocks that grow with the iteration count for a
# live set of one map with one key.
#
# This golden exists to be regenerated. When a threaded container's storage
# moves out of the rewound region, the bracket can be emitted and these
# numbers fall — and the diff is the evidence that it worked.
fn go 0 m
  print (length (entries m))

fn go n m
  junk = "k{n % 17}"
  go (n - 1) (put m "k1" (m["k1"]! + length junk))

pub play = go 20000 { "k1":0 }
